<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sa'd al-Dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 991</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Abu'l-Ma'ali Sharif</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sa'd al-Dawla was the Hamdanid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> from 967 to 991, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a>. Much of his reign was spent in struggles with rebellious commanders and between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, to which Aleppo paid tribute from 969, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-al-dawla">Sa'id al-Dawla</a> succeeded him under the control of the chamberlain Lu'lu'.</p></div>
